Use a 128 MB structure heap on QNX.

JSC reserves its structure heap with tryReserveUncommittedAligned(size,
structureHeapAddressSize).  StructureMemoryManager halves the size up to eight
times when the reservation fails, but the *alignment* argument stays at
structureHeapAddressSize -- so with the 4 GB default every attempt needs up to
8 GB of contiguous address space.  On a QNX target with 1 GB of RAM all eight
fail and the RELEASE_ASSERT aborts inside JSContextGroupCreate(), before any
script runs.  It passes on an 8 GB board, so the failure looks like an
architecture difference and is really an address-space one.

128 MB is the value PLAYSTATION already uses for the same reason.  StructureID
derives its masks from this constant, so nothing else needs changing.

Upstream-Status: QNX-specific

--- a/Source/JavaScriptCore/runtime/StructureID.h
+++ b/Source/JavaScriptCore/runtime/StructureID.h
@@ -49,6 +49,14 @@
 constexpr uintptr_t structureHeapAddressSize = STRUCTURE_HEAP_ADDRESS_SIZE_IN_MB * MB;
 #elif PLATFORM(PLAYSTATION)
 constexpr uintptr_t structureHeapAddressSize = 128 * MB;
+#elif OS(QNX)
+// The reservation below is aligned to structureHeapAddressSize, and that
+// alignment is not reduced when the size is halved on failure -- so a 4 GB
+// value asks for up to 8 GB of address space.  A QNX target with 1 GB of RAM
+// cannot satisfy it, every attempt in StructureMemoryManager fails, and the
+// RELEASE_ASSERT aborts inside JSContextGroupCreate().  128 MB is what
+// PLAYSTATION uses for the same reason.
+constexpr uintptr_t structureHeapAddressSize = 128 * MB;
 #elif PLATFORM(IOS_FAMILY) && CPU(ARM64) && !CPU(ARM64E)
 constexpr uintptr_t structureHeapAddressSize = 512 * MB;
 #else
